    "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\n`vim.fs.normalize()` will always expand a leading `~` to the user's home directory. Since files can be named `~`, this can be a problem when a relative path is passed in.\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nAdd an `expand_home` option to `vim.fs.normalize()` in the same vein as `expand_env`.\r\n\r\nI also thought about just converting `expand_env` to a generalized `expand` option which controls both, since I imagine that these options will typically be set together. I didn't want to make a breaking change here, but if you think it's worth it for simplicity's sake (`~` is similar to the env `$HOME`, after all), I can change this PR to do that.\r\n\r\nAlso added a test for `expend_env` since there wasn't one for some reason.",
    "closed_at": null,
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I agree with keeping this new setting separate from `expand_env`, but I do wonder if maybe we should generalize it further to not expand escaped characters.\r\n\r\n`expand_env` should perhaps have been named `plain` instead. maybe we should do that here, and deprecate `expand_env`.\r\n",
